  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "Given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}-9x+21}{(x-4)^{2}(3-x)}=\\frac{A}{x-4}+\\frac{B}{(x-4)^{2}}+\\frac{C}{3-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$, $B$, and $C$ are real constants, determine the value of $A\\cdot B\\cdot C.$",
  "worked_solution": "Multiplying both sides by $(x-4)^{2}(3-x)$ gives\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-9x+21=A(x-4)(3-x)+B(3-x)+C(x-4)^{2}.\n$$\n\nLetting $x=4$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{2}-9(4)+21 & =A(4-4)(3-4)+B(3-4)+C(4-4)^{2} \\\\ 16-36+21 & =B(-1) \\\\ 1 & =-B \\\\ B & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=3$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{2}-9(3)+21 & =A(3-4)(3-3)+B(3-3)+C(3-4)^{2} \\\\ 9-27+21 & =C{(-1)}^{2} \\\\ C & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, letting $x=0$ and using our values for $B$ and $C$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}-9(0)+21 & =A(0-4)(3-0)+(-1)(3-0)+3(0-4)^{2} \\\\ 21 & =-12A-3+48 \\\\ 21 & =-12A+45 \\\\ 12A & =24 \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nA\\cdot B\\cdot C=2\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 3=-6.\n$$",
